  "tail": "\n**2. Is the quoted sentence true, false, or ambiguous?**\n\nThe sentence:  \n> “it holds only at 0, at 1, and at those ordinals that are already infinite cardinals.”\n\nThe word **“only”** modifies the whole list. In standard mathematical English the sentence asserts that the condition cf(α)=α is true **exactly** for the ordinals 0, 1, and all infinite cardinals. This claims both a necessary condition (if it holds then the ordinal must be 0, 1 or an infinite cardinal) and a sufficient condition (every ordinal that is 0, 1 or an infinite cardinal satisfies it).\n\n- The **necessary** part is true: any α with cf(α)=α is indeed 0, 1 or an infinite cardinal (by `thm-cofinality-basics`).\n- The **sufficient** part is **false**, because singular infinite cardinals (e.g. ℵ_ω) are infinite cardinals yet do **not** satisfy cf(κ)=κ.\n\nTherefore the sentence as written is **false**. A competent reader of this page, having already seen the definition of “singular” (cf(κ)≠κ) and the statement that ℵ_ω is singular (`thm-regularity-of-the-alephs`), would take the natural reading and recognise the error.\n\n---\n\n**3. Does the sentence contradict anything else on this page?**\n\nYes, it contradicts three places on the same page:\n\n- The **Definition section** itself: “An infinite cardinal κ … is **singular** when cf(κ) ≠ κ.” This directly implies that some infinite cardinals do **not** satisfy cf(κ)=κ.\n- The **second Remark**: “A cardinal that is reachable from below by fewer than κ steps … That is exactly the failure of regularity”. Failure of regularity means cf(κ) ≠ κ, again showing that not all infinite cardinals satisfy the condition.\n- **`thm-regularity-of-the-alephs`** (c), quoted verbatim: “In ZF. cf(ℵ_ω) = ℵ_0, and ℵ_0 < ℵ_ω, so ℵ_ω is **singular**.” This gives a concrete infinite cardinal that does **not** satisfy cf(ℵ_ω)=ℵ_ω.\n\nThus the sentence’s claim that the condition holds for all infinite cardinals is inconsistent with the very page it appears on.\n\n---\n\n## Findings\n\n### FINDING 1 — FATAL — location: Remark 1 (first Remark)\nCLAIM AS WRITTEN: “it holds only at 0, at 1, and at those ordinals that are already infinite cardinals.”\nWHY IT FAILS: This asserts that the condition cf(α)=α is true for every infinite cardinal. However, the item itself defines **singular** infinite cardinals by cf(κ) ≠ κ, and the neighbouring theorem `thm-regularity-of-the-alephs` (c) proves that ℵ_ω is such a singular infinite cardinal (cf(ℵ_ω)=ℵ_0 ≠ ℵ_ω). Hence the condition does **not** hold at all infinite cardinals; the stated enumeration is mathematically false.\nWHAT WOULD FIX IT: Replace “those ordinals that are already infinite cardinals” with “those infinite cardinals that are regular (i.e., satisfy cf(κ)=κ)” — for example: “it holds only at 0, at 1, and at those infinite cardinals that are regular”.\n\n### FINDING 2 — NONFATAL — location: Remark 4 (fourth Remark)\nCLAIM AS WRITTEN: “Only one notion of ‘cofinal’ exists in this library.”\nWHY IT FAILS: The provided dependencies cover only the items explicitly cited; they do not constitute the entire library. It is impossible to verify that no other page introduces a different notion of “cofinal” (for partial orders, directed sets, filters, etc.). The claim is therefore unverifiable with the texts at hand. (The local claims about `def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal` not introducing cofinality or the regular/singular vocabulary are consistent with the quoted text of that item.)\nWHAT WOULD FIX IT: Either remove the global claim or restrict it to the items that can be checked, e.g., “The only notion of ‘cofinal’ introduced in the items directly relevant to this page is the one from `def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal`.”\n\n1 fatal, 1 nonfatal"
